**Read:** [Introdução à Mecânica dos Fluidos e aos Fenômenos de Transporte — Gobbi and colleagues](../sources/Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J), chapters 3 and 8 material descriptions and derivative; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 1 descriptions of motion and material derivative; [Differential Equations — MIT 18.03SC](../../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Differential-Equations-MIT-18-03SC.lingua#r_1VJHPM3DQ0PWYNFYKD11EKGCZE), ordinary differential equations needed to follow a trajectory.

A velocity field u(x,t) reports velocity at a position and time. A moving material point follows dx/dt=u(x,t). Its acceleration includes change at a fixed position and change caused by entering another part of the field: Du/Dt=∂u/∂t+(u·∇)u.

Consider the steady planar field u=(a x,−a y,0), with constant a. A particle follows x=x₀ exp(at), y=y₀ exp(−at). Its acceleration is (a²x,a²y,0), although the local time derivative is zero. Area is preserved while a material element stretches in one direction and contracts in the other.

Write the units of a and verify the trajectories by differentiating them. Compare a fixed sensor's time series with the history of one moving particle. Neither description is more physical; they answer different questions and must be transformed consistently. The same distinction matters when a fluid carries temperature or composition through a nonuniform field.

